WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.049
Hi guys! Dan here for BBC Learning English
with this week's Learner Question. Find out

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.100
what it is after this.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:23.420
OK! This week's learner question comes from
Oum in Cambodia, who asks "When should I use

00:00:23.429 --> 00:00:29.199
the words bored and boring?" It's a very good
question Oum. Are you listening? Here we go.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.450
Bored and boring are both adjectives. They
have the same meaning i.e. not interesting.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:39.880
But they're used in different ways. Consider:
I am bored because this TV programme is boring,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.149
or, this film is frightening so I am frightened.

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:48.660
In English, when an adjective has an -ed
and an -ing form. The -ed form describes the

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.949
feeling of the person, whereas the -ing form
describes the object which makes us feel.

00:00:54.949 --> 00:01:01.210
I feel bored because the TV is boring. I feel
frightened because the film is frightening.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:07.400
But if I stop watching the film, I will not
be frightened any more, yet the film will

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.670
still be frightening.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.120
We can make another difference between bored
and boring - one which describes a temporary

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:21.180
feeling or a permanent quality. For example:
I don't like David. He's boring. Or: I'm bored

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.781
let's go and have some fun. In the first example
I don't like David because he's boring. This

00:01:26.781 --> 00:01:31.740
describes a permanent quality. David was boring
yesterday, he's boring today, and he will

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:37.130
probably be boring tomorrow. However, in my
second example, I have a temporary feeling.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:42.170
I am bored. If I go and do something fun I
won't be bored any more, will I?

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.750
I hope that answers your question Oum. Thank
you very much for writing to us. If anybody

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:53.110
else out there has a Learners' Questions,
please email us on: learning.english@bbc.co.uk.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.930
Please remember to include Learners' Questions
in the subject box and your name and the country

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.810
where you come from. We can't possibly answer
all the questions because there are so, so

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.680
many, but we do read every single one. And
for more information about this or anything

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.800
else, you can go to our website: bbclearningenglish.com.
See you next time on Learners' Questions.

